  <section class="mbc-sec" id="mbc-what">
    <h2>What Is a Male Body Type Calculator and How Does It Work?</h2>
    <p>A male body type calculator compares your chest, waist and hip circumferences to classify your build into one of five recognised shapes. It's the same underlying logic used in our <a href="/">body type calculator</a> for women — comparing the width of your upper body against your hips, then checking how much your waist tapers between them — just calibrated around male proportions.</p>
    <p>Chest and hips are compared first to see which is wider and by how much, then the waist is checked against the chest to see how defined it is. That places you into one of five shapes: trapezoid, inverted triangle, rectangle, triangle or oval.</p>
    <div class="mbc-note">
      <p><strong>This is a styling and self-awareness tool, not a health screening device.</strong> It's a starting point for understanding your proportions and dressing for them — not a medical classification.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mbc-sec" id="mbc-measure">
    <h2>How to Measure Chest, Waist and Hips</h2>
    <p>Use a soft fabric tape, stand naturally with arms relaxed, and breathe normally rather than puffing your chest out or pulling your stomach in.</p>

    <h3>Chest</h3>
    <p>Wrap the tape around the fullest part of your chest, under the arms and across the shoulder blades. Keep it level front to back and snug without compressing anything.</p>

    <h3>Waist</h3>
    <p>Measure at your natural waist — roughly at the navel, the narrowest point of your torso for most men. Exhale gently and don't suck in; you want your resting measurement.</p>

    <h3>Hips</h3>
    <p>Stand with feet together and measure around the widest part of your seat, keeping the tape level all the way around.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="mbc-sec" id="mbc-shapes">
    <h2>The 5 Male Body Shapes Explained (With Formulas)</h2>
    <p>Male shape classification typically uses five categories built around one core comparison: how much wider your chest is than your hips, and how much your waist tapers in between.</p>

    <div class="mbc-tbl-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Shape</th><th scope="col">Defining formula (inches)</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Trapezoid</td><td>Chest ≥ 3 in wider than hips; chest−waist ≥ 7 in</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Inverted Triangle</td><td>Chest ≥ 3 in wider than hips; chest−waist &lt; 7 in</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Triangle</td><td>Hips ≥ 3 in wider than chest</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Oval</td><td>Chest &amp; hips within 3 in of each other; waist within 2 in of — or larger than — both</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Rectangle</td><td>Catch-all: chest and hips close, waist moderately smaller than both</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3>Trapezoid — Broad Chest, Tapered Waist</h3>
    <p>Widely considered the classic athletic male build: chest and shoulders clearly wider than the hips, with a waist that tapers in noticeably between them — at least 7 inches smaller than the chest. This is the V-shape most strength-training advice is implicitly aimed at. If this is your result, your proportions already read as athletic; the styling goal is simply not to hide that taper under boxy fabric.</p>

    <h3>Inverted Triangle — Broad Chest, Straight Waist</h3>
    <p>Similar chest-to-hip spread as a trapezoid, but without the same waist taper — chest−waist comes in under 7 inches. The silhouette is broad and boxy through the torso rather than V-shaped. This is common in bigger, huskier builds and in men who carry muscle through the trunk without a sharply defined waist.</p>

    <h3>Triangle — Narrower Chest, Wider Hips</h3>
    <p>Hips at least 3 inches wider than the chest — narrower shoulders with fullness sitting in the hips and thighs. Less commonly discussed than the female pear shape, but the same styling logic applies: the goal is adding visual width up top to balance the frame.</p>

    <h3>Oval — Fuller Midsection</h3>
    <p>Chest and hips are close in size, but the waist doesn't taper — it sits close to, or even exceeds, both. This is the shape colloquially called a "dad bod" or "beer belly" build. Like the female apple shape, <strong>oval isn't a fitness judgment</strong> — it describes where a frame carries fullness, and it can show up at a wide range of overall sizes.</p>

    <h3>Rectangle — Straight Up and Down</h3>
    <p>Chest, waist and hips are all fairly close, with the waist somewhat smaller than the chest but not dramatically so. A lean, uniform silhouette without pronounced taper in either direction — common in naturally slim, narrow-framed builds.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="mbc-sec" id="mbc-style">
    <h2>How to Dress for Your Male Body Type</h2>
    <p>The general principle across all five shapes: fit matters more than any single style choice, and the goal is proportion, not disguise.</p>

    <h3>Dressing for a Trapezoid Build</h3>
    <p>Show the taper rather than hiding it — fitted (not skin-tight) shirts, tailored blazers that follow the waist, and flat-front trousers all work with your proportions instead of against them.</p>

    <h3>Dressing for an Inverted Triangle Build</h3>
    <p>A single-breasted jacket with minimal shoulder padding keeps the upper body from reading as boxier than it needs to. Straight or slightly relaxed trousers balance a broad torso better than skinny cuts, which exaggerate the width up top.</p>

    <h3>Dressing for a Triangle Build</h3>
    <p>Structured jackets and shirts with some texture or pattern on top add visual width to the shoulders. Slim, tapered trousers in darker tones keep the lower half from adding further width.</p>

    <h3>Dressing for an Oval Build</h3>
    <p>Vertical lines — pinstripes, open jackets worn as a layer, shirts with a defined placket — elongate the torso. Avoid tucking in tightly fitted shirts across the midsection; a slightly longer, straighter cut skims rather than clings. Flat-front trousers sit better than pleated ones, which add bulk exactly where you don't want it.</p>

    <h3>Dressing for a Rectangle Build</h3>
    <p>Layering — a jacket over a shirt, texture on texture — adds the visual dimension a straight frame doesn't naturally have. Structured shoulders and a slightly fitted waist on jackets create the impression of a more defined silhouette.</p>

    <h3>What to Wear for Formal Occasions by Body Shape</h3>
    <div class="mbc-tbl-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Shape</th><th scope="col">Formal go-to</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Trapezoid</td><td>A well-tailored two-piece suit that follows the natural taper — this build carries a fitted cut better than almost any other</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Inverted Triangle</td><td>Single-breasted suit, minimal shoulder structure, straight-leg trousers</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Triangle</td><td>Structured-shoulder blazer with a patterned or textured tie to draw the eye upward</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Oval</td><td>Single-breasted, single vent, dark solid colours with a vertical stripe if patterned</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Rectangle</td><td>Double-breasted or structured-shoulder suit to add shape a straight build doesn't provide on its own</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mbc-sec" id="mbc-measure2">
    <h2>Common Male Body Shape Measurements by Size</h2>
    <p>The formulas hold at every size — it's the relationship between the numbers that defines the shape, not their absolute size:</p>
    <div class="mbc-tbl-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Shape</th><th scope="col">Chest</th><th scope="col">Waist</th><th scope="col">Hips</th></tr></thead>
