          <tr><td>Triangle</td><td>38 in</td><td>36 in</td><td>42 in</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Oval</td><td>40 in</td><td>42 in</td><td>39 in</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Rectangle</td><td>40 in</td><td>37 in</td><td>40 in</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mbc-sec" id="mbc-whr">
    <h2>Body Shape and Waist-to-Hip Ratio</h2>
    <p>Since this calculator already collects your waist and hip measurements, it's a short step to your waist-to-hip ratio (WHR) — shown above alongside your shape result. The WHO's threshold for men is 0.90; above that line is classified as abdominal obesity and linked to higher cardiovascular risk. An oval build tends to run highest on this measure, for the same reason an apple shape does for women — the waist is the fullest point rather than the hips. Run the numbers on their own on our dedicated <a href="/waist-to-hip-ratio-calculator/">waist to hip ratio calculator</a> for the full risk breakdown and sourcing.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="mbc-sec" id="mbc-somatotype">
    <h2>Body Shape vs Somatotype — What's the Difference?</h2>
    <p>Body shape (the trapezoid/inverted-triangle/rectangle/triangle/oval system above) and somatotype (ectomorph/mesomorph/endomorph) are two different, complementary frameworks — one measurement-based, one built around frame and metabolism rather than a tape measure. They're not interchangeable: a mesomorph, for instance, usually measures as a trapezoid or inverted triangle, but the somatotype label describes how your body tends to respond to training and diet, not your current proportions. Our <a href="/">body type calculator</a> covers the somatotype system in more depth if you want both angles.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="mbc-sec" id="mbc-faq">
    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

    <details class="mbc-faq"><summary>What are the 5 male body types?</summary><div class="mbc-faq-body">
      <p>Trapezoid, inverted triangle, rectangle, triangle and oval — based on comparing chest, waist and hip measurements. Trapezoid and inverted triangle both have a chest wider than the hips; the difference is how much the waist tapers in between.</p>
    </div></details>

    <details class="mbc-faq"><summary>What's the difference between trapezoid and inverted triangle?</summary><div class="mbc-faq-body">
      <p>Both have a chest at least 3 inches wider than the hips. In a trapezoid, the waist tapers in by 7 inches or more from the chest — a visible V-shape. In an inverted triangle, the waist doesn't taper as much, giving a broader, boxier torso without the same waist definition.</p>
    </div></details>

    <details class="mbc-faq"><summary>Is oval the same as a "dad bod"?</summary><div class="mbc-faq-body">
      <p>They overlap but aren't identical terms. "Dad bod" is a casual, informal description; oval is the measurement-based category it usually falls into — chest and hips fairly close in size, with a waist that doesn't taper between them. Like any shape, it isn't tied to a specific fitness level or body size.</p>
    </div></details>

    <details class="mbc-faq"><summary>Can your body shape change with training?</summary><div class="mbc-faq-body">
      <p>Yes, more so than the female shape system in some ways — building shoulder, back and chest muscle can genuinely widen your chest-to-hip gap over time and shift a rectangle toward an inverted triangle or trapezoid. Waist size responds to overall body composition rather than targeted exercise. Hip width, driven mostly by bone structure, changes the least.</p>
    </div></details>

    <details class="mbc-faq"><summary>How is this different from the ectomorph/mesomorph/endomorph system?</summary><div class="mbc-faq-body">
      <p>Somatotype describes your general frame and how your body tends to respond to training and diet — it's a broader, less precise classification. Body shape, measured here, is a direct comparison of your current chest, waist and hip numbers. They're related but answer different questions; see the section above for more detail.</p>
    </div></details>

    <details class="mbc-faq"><summary>How often should I re-measure?</summary><div class="mbc-faq-body">
      <p>Every six months is enough for most people, or after a significant change in training, weight or age-related shifts. Measuring more often mostly captures normal daily fluctuation rather than a real change in proportion.</p>
    </div></details>
  </section>

  <p class="mbc-disc">This calculator is an educational and styling tool. It does not diagnose any medical condition and its results are not a substitute for advice from a qualified healthcare professional. If you have concerns about your weight, waist-to-hip ratio or general health, speak to a doctor.</p>

